\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces  A three-panel collage illustrating diverse swim patterns generated by the swim CPG circuit, adapting to the maturing size of Dendronotus iris. (A) Swim CPG circuitry featuring core interneurons Si2L/R and Si3L/R, driven by the sensory interneurons Si1s. (B) Voltage traces (top) and neurotransmitter release probabilities for the four core swim CPG interneurons (SiN). (C) As {\em  Dendronotus Iris} grows from 30g to 300g over its lifespan, as shown in the voltage traces in panel\nobreakspace  {}B. its swim period slows down from 2--3s in juveniles to up to 12--14 seconds in older adults, see the voltage traces in panel\nobreakspace  {}B.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:group_fig5}{{1}{3}{A three-panel collage illustrating diverse swim patterns generated by the swim CPG circuit, adapting to the maturing size of Dendronotus iris. (A) Swim CPG circuitry featuring core interneurons Si2L/R and Si3L/R, driven by the sensory interneurons Si1s. (B) Voltage traces (top) and neurotransmitter release probabilities for the four core swim CPG interneurons (SiN). (C) As {\em Dendronotus Iris} grows from 30g to 300g over its lifespan, as shown in the voltage traces in panel~B. its swim period slows down from 2--3s in juveniles to up to 12--14 seconds in older adults, see the voltage traces in panel~B}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces  Anti-phase left–right episodes within a swim pattern, produced by the Dendronotus iris CPG under control conditions, arise from twisted pairwise modules (Si3R $\leftrightarrows $ Si2L and Si3L $\leftrightarrows $ Si2R) that are unidirectionally coupled via excitatory ($\blacktriangledown $) and inhibitory ($\bullet $) synapses. These modules alternate in initiating the onset of network bursting, an effect further reinforced by two reciprocally inhibitory HSOs, consisting respectively of Si3R–Si3L and Si2R–Si2L pairs. Compare with the original Fig.1\nobreakspace  {}from the experimental study\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {sakurai2022bursting}. }}{3}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:group_fig5}{{2}{3}{Anti-phase left–right episodes within a swim pattern, produced by the Dendronotus iris CPG under control conditions, arise from twisted pairwise modules (Si3R $\leftrightarrows $ Si2L and Si3L $\leftrightarrows $ Si2R) that are unidirectionally coupled via excitatory ($\blacktriangledown $) and inhibitory ($\bullet $) synapses. These modules alternate in initiating the onset of network bursting, an effect further reinforced by two reciprocally inhibitory HSOs, consisting respectively of Si3R–Si3L and Si2R–Si2L pairs. Compare with the original Fig.1~from the experimental study~\cite {sakurai2022bursting}}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Comparison of specific, distinct responses of the swim CPG to specific perturbations using hyper- and depolarized current pulses (indicated by red vertical arrows) injected into the targeted interneurons let us validate the CPG model against its biological original and demonstrate the resilience of both to restore and proceed with the normal swim patterns. Fact check list\nobreakspace  {}I: to compare these six modeling panels with the corresponding experimental originals: Figs.\nobreakspace  {}5A--C and E--G, resp. from Ref.\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {SakuraiKatz2016}. For the empirical resilience evidence see also Fig.\nobreakspace  {}5 in Ref.\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {SakuraiKatz2017}; for the PIR de-facto proof see Fig.\nobreakspace  {}5 in the experimental Ref.\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {sakurai2022bursting}.}}{4}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:group_fig5}{{3}{4}{Comparison of specific, distinct responses of the swim CPG to specific perturbations using hyper- and depolarized current pulses (indicated by red vertical arrows) injected into the targeted interneurons let us validate the CPG model against its biological original and demonstrate the resilience of both to restore and proceed with the normal swim patterns. Fact check list~I: to compare these six modeling panels with the corresponding experimental originals: Figs.~5A--C and E--G, resp. from Ref.~\cite {SakuraiKatz2016}. For the empirical resilience evidence see also Fig.~5 in Ref.~\cite {SakuraiKatz2017}; for the PIR de-facto proof see Fig.~5 in the experimental Ref.~\cite {sakurai2022bursting}}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Similar responses of the swim CPG in control swim to various temporal perturbations using hyper- and depolarized current pulses (indicated by red vertical arrows) applied to the targeted interneurons let us validate the CPG model against its biological originals Fact check list\nobreakspace  {}II: to compare these four modeling panels with the corresponding experimental originals: Figs.\nobreakspace  {}7Ai--Di from Ref.\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {SakuraiKatz2016}}}{4}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:group_fig7}{{4}{4}{Similar responses of the swim CPG in control swim to various temporal perturbations using hyper- and depolarized current pulses (indicated by red vertical arrows) applied to the targeted interneurons let us validate the CPG model against its biological originals Fact check list~II: to compare these four modeling panels with the corresponding experimental originals: Figs.~7Ai--Di from Ref.~\cite {SakuraiKatz2016}}{figure.4}{}}
