\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Fact check IV to compare these panels\nobreakspace  {}B and C with the original neurophysiological data -- Fig.\nobreakspace  {}7 in Ref.\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {sakurai2022bursting}. Three positive current pulses of increasing amplitude are applied to Si2R to calibrate $\alpha $-synapse and logistic synapse (bottom trace) models and IPSP responses in the counterlateral Si2L (top trace). Two positive current pulses injected into Si2R trigger accumulating IPSP responses in Si2L (top traces) and fast and slow echoes in the Si3L due to fast gap junction and delayed slow inhibition due to the logistic synapse between Si2R and Si3L, see the swim CPG circuitry.}}{6}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:group_fig5}{{6}{6}{Fact check IV to compare these panels~B and C with the original neurophysiological data -- Fig.~7 in Ref.~\cite {sakurai2022bursting}. Three positive current pulses of increasing amplitude are applied to Si2R to calibrate $\alpha $-synapse and logistic synapse (bottom trace) models and IPSP responses in the counterlateral Si2L (top trace). Two positive current pulses injected into Si2R trigger accumulating IPSP responses in Si2L (top traces) and fast and slow echoes in the Si3L due to fast gap junction and delayed slow inhibition due to the logistic synapse between Si2R and Si3L, see the swim CPG circuitry}{figure.6}{}}
\newlabel{LastBibItem}{{23}{6}{}{section*.15}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces CPG assembly line in five sequential steps [in arbitrary order]. (A) Uncoupled four interneurons, with Si3's being at higher spike rate due to ``excitatory drive'' from Si1's. (B) Si3's drive ipsilaterally Si2's with fast excitatory drive. (C) Si2's return inhibitory feedback onto Si3's. (D) Reciprocally inhibitory synapses are turned on between contralateral Si3's. (D) Reciprocally inhibitory synapses are turned on between contralateral Si2's, along with gap junctions between Si3's and Si3's, to generate stably the fast network-level bursting in the swim CPG model of {\em  Dendronotus Iris}. }}{7}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:group_fig5}{{7}{7}{CPG assembly line in five sequential steps [in arbitrary order]. (A) Uncoupled four interneurons, with Si3's being at higher spike rate due to ``excitatory drive'' from Si1's. (B) Si3's drive ipsilaterally Si2's with fast excitatory drive. (C) Si2's return inhibitory feedback onto Si3's. (D) Reciprocally inhibitory synapses are turned on between contralateral Si3's. (D) Reciprocally inhibitory synapses are turned on between contralateral Si2's, along with gap junctions between Si3's and Si3's, to generate stably the fast network-level bursting in the swim CPG model of {\em Dendronotus Iris}}{figure.7}{}}
